comments.body:
@eqy Thanks for the clarification. I understand that TF32 can introduce larger numerical differences, and I agree that this is important to separate from a pure compiler issue.

That said, my main concern is the end-to-end behavior: when switching execution modes, I observed inconsistent predicted labels. From my perspective, the important question is not only whether the per-layer difference can be explained by TF32, but also whether this difference is expected to propagate and change the final model output under torch.compile.

I can further clarify the repro with TF32 settings made explicit, but I would also appreciate guidance on whether this kind of prediction inconsistency should be considered expected behavior when TF32 is enabled.

In the python case, you need the xformers wheel installed.

@bhack, it sounds like what you're asking for is for us to include the custom op code directly into the model.so.

I think we should add support for this mode of operation. However, this would likely require a step where AOTI needs the source code of the custom op and compiles it into the model.so. Do you think that's fine?
